#passage(("You see first how the Islamic Empire was: that it was connected with the rest of the world. There was trading systems going on, there was multiculturalism going on, there's sharing of values and ideas and cultures and understandings between the people. But what's more important, as he says, that Muslims, if they take over a country or take over a civilisation, they would take existing ideas and they would let them flourish. They would take existing resources, they wouldn't take them out to themselves. They would leave the existing resources and they would flourish that land and that country, and they would allow people to practice their Faith in harmony. That is the difference between Muslims and the other religions or other ideologies when they conquer and take over lands.", "When we look at the Western conquest, or when we look at the Christian, when they took over people's land, or we look at Western empires or American empires, any of these empires, whenever they take over land, what they do is they plunder all of the resources that is in that land, in that country, and they take it to themselves and they allow their country to advance. And they take the knowledge and everything within that country, and then they leave that country for destruction. They destroy the infrastructure of the country. They take everything within it and they try to become superior, while any place that they went in before, or any country, they make it inferior, and they completely destroy it. So the difference between Islamic 'expansion', if you want to use that term, and Western expansion is that.", "A lot of people come and say, when Muslims talk about what Westerners do to Middle Eastern countries or generally even African countries or any countries when they take over these lands, they say: Muslims are complaining, but look at your empire, what you did! Yes, but when Muslims expanded, they did not destroy countries and plunder their resources and destroy the infrastructure and left. Give me an example of Muslims doing that. That wasn't something that Muslims were doing. Look at Baghdad as an example: the capital, the MAJOR capital of the world, or the capital also of the Muslim empire at the time. It was the center of civilisation in the world. That was a place that Muslims took and flourished.", "Muslims had this idea of the House of Wisdom or بَيتُ الحِكمة (Baytul Hikma), where you would have translation of literature from other languages, you'd have discussions, you'd have flourishing of science. A lot of the people in the West are like: oh, look, Muslims, they took over, there's no free speech, there's no education, there's no science. When you look at Islamic history, when Muslims were in power, they allowed knowledge to flourish. They translated foreign knowledge, they experimented, and allowed science and knowledge in general to flourish. This is a clear refutation of this idea that Muslims were anti-free speech, anti-discussion, anti-dialogue between different ideologies.", "He mentions people like Ibn Rushd, people like Ibn Sina, alKhawarizmi, Ibn alHaytham. He mentions so many people, but he didn't mention why they were translating or Latinising their names and changing their names into Avicenna and Averroes and Algorithmi. It's not just simply because it was a different language. It's not that, because you can type the letters in your own language and you can still say the name. The main reason they were doing that is because this idea of the History and civilisation of the West being stolen from the Muslims, they had to cover it up somehow! So if they go and they steal knowledge, going to Baghdad and learning knowledge from Muslims there, or people who were within the Islamic civilisation, physicians or otherwise, like you have Ibn Sina and you have all of these people: you have their books and editions being taught in books of physicians today in the biggest schools in the UK and America. You have statues of people like arRazi in France, I believe, and other places.", "And they latinise and they change their names because they don't want people to understand that, look, the source of this knowledge are actually Arabic names or Muslim names! That this knowledge actually came from Muslims. So if I change the name, a student reading wouldn't clue this guy's a Muslim. If you go to most of these people in these universities, being taught this knowledge, [with 16] different editions when it comes to physician and knowledge of medicine, taken from Ibn Sina, you ask them: who's Avicenna? They'll say: oh, he was a Greek person. No, he wasn't Greek! They just latinised the name, changed it to Latin and Greek. So when you're reading it, you think: oh, all of this knowledge actually comes from the Greeks, all of this knowledge actually comes from \"our people\", the Roman Empire accepting Christianity, rather than it actually came from the Islamic Golden Age.", "He's talking about alKhawarizmi. 'Algorithm', or the idea of algorithms, and this computer we're using, and laptops, and all of this stuff, and algebra as an entire science, are coming from Muslims, coming from someone like that. Most people don't know! They don't know the word 'algorithm', where does it come from and where does it trace back to. So many people don't know this, and it's really sad!",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Responding To Professor Jiang's Lecture On The Islamic Golden Age | Muhammed Ali!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SVGKQ-xiGs", ((1122, "18:42–19:18"), (1161, "19:21–20:01"), (1202, "20:02–20:37"), (1346, "22:26–23:16"), (1513, "25:13–26:06"), (1572, "26:12–26:50"), (1634, "27:14–27:40"),), id: 3123)
