#passage(("That's historically inaccurate. In Islam, it's not like Catholicism: a person who was called the Pope, and what they say is infallible, and whatever he says goes; and the priests are infallible, the Church's father is infallible, and what they say goes! That's not Islam; this is more like a Catholicism that you're imposing on Islam. Even if a great scholar comes at Islamic history and says whatever he says, even if he's a leader (a leader is even less, because scholars would be taking over a higher position as a religious authority over a leader), what you said there is not only inaccurate historically, it's also unreasonable! Because Muslims do not base the religion on what people say to be, as you guys use the term, gospel truth. We don't take teachings of a Scholar or a King or Anyone to be revelation from God, that if he said so everyone is going to do this. So the whole civilisation of Islam is not going to change because one individual, one leader said whatever it is.", "Now, what happened is that people have started moving away from their own religion. People have started to drink, there was fornication, there was adultery. This is what started to happen. In the end, in the decline of what was called the Ottoman Empire, they started fighting with their own. The Arabs started fighting with the Ottomans, and they had the Brits, and they were helping them. That was the reason for the end of the Ottoman Empire in that period: people moving away from the religion and allying enemies over their own Brothers in Faith, and fighting against them, is what led to the demise of the Empire at the time. So that has nothing to do with just one person coming and saying something.", "If you look at the Ottoman Empire remaining for over 600 years, it's one of the longest; you have no other examples to demonstrate right now that is similar to it today. It was, as you said, a lot of advancements. You got Avicenna (Ibn Sina); even though we don't necessarily agree with