#passage(("We have this Egyptian Coptic Christian. He's going to tell us about what Muslims did when they conquered Egypt. I genuinely believe that some of the Coptic Christians (I'm not going to say all of them, because I had encounters with some of them which were good), a lot of the Coptic Christians are some of the most ungrateful people you're going to see in your entire life. I'm going to have now to give this guy an entire history lesson from his own books, to actually see what happened when Muslims went to Egypt.", "Muslims conquered Egypt, which was specifically Amr ibn al-As, the companion of Prophet Muhammad, when he went to Egypt. We have this book called the Synaxarium, the book of saints. It's the central text that records the lives of the martyrs and the key church events that happened in the Coptic Church. Now, he said when Muslims went to Egypt, they massacred everyone, they destroyed the churches, they killed the women, the children, all of that. I'm not going to read from an Islamic source of history. I'm going to read from that book, which is their own book of Coptic Church history. Let's read in the section that specifically talks about the Pope. Pope at the time was called Benjamin. Before the Islamic conquest, it was the Roman Empire, and Heraclius, the Roman Emperor, he sent Cyrus to govern over Alexandria in Egypt.", "The book says: when Heraclius, the Roman Emperor, took over the country, Egypt and Alexandria, he appointed Cyrus as a patriarch and the governor of Alexandria. He persecuted the Orthodox Coptic Christians. He was seeking to force them to accept the Tome of Leo and the Council of Chalcedon. He tortured the believers, killing many and burning others. Because of this, the Pope, Benjamin the 38th, who was the patriarch at that time of the Coptics, he fled to the wilderness, and he remained hidden in the Upper Egyptian monasteries for 13 years. So, now, what do we have? We have Christians, the Roman Empire, and all of the killing that he was talking about, and killing children and destroying churches: all of this stuff that he's attributing to Muslims is actually Christians are doing that. I'm showing you Christians are doing them to each other, but not only they're doing it to each other, they're obviously doing it to the Muslims as well.", "It also records that Cyrus, he [seized] Mina. Who is Mina? He was the brother of the Pope. He tortured him severely. He brought him before a great fire and he said: \"Acknowledge the council and I will let you go.\" Then Mina replied: \"I do not fear your fire, but I fear the eternal fire,\" meaning the eternal fire of the afterlife. What did Cyrus do? Cyrus became enraged and he ordered his soldiers to bring a large torch and apply it to the sides of Mina until the fat of his body started dropping on the ground. Then he ordered his teeth to be knocked out. And then he ordered him to be placed on a sack filled with sand and drowned into the sea.", "What was happening to the churches? The churches at the time, this is what the book says: the churches has been deserted, and the Melkites, obviously referring to the people who are from the Roman Empire, they took the position of the great church of Alexandria and the coastal sites. Many of the sanctuaries were in ruins and the monks had fled from the [monasteries]. They ran away because of the oppression of the Roman governors. And then it talks about the return of Benjamin. When Benjamin returned, he found the church of the Saint Mark of Alexandria destroyed and the body of the apostle missing from its place.", "I'm going to tell you now why I'm calling him ungrateful, because this was the state of Egypt before Amr ibn al-As. The state was that we had the Roman Empire and Cyrus persecuting and killing the Coptic Christians, completely destroying them, demolishing them, doing whatever he wanted. This is what he was doing to the churches. They were in complete ruins. Then who came? Amr ibn al-As came, and he fought off Cyrus and defeated him. What does the book, the Coptic Church Book of History, record? Amr ibn al-As had this agreement, and this is what was written: \"In the name of God, the most compassionate, the most merciful. This is the protection which Amr ibn al-As grants to the people of Egypt for their lives, their religion, their property, their churches, their crosses, their land, their waters. Nothing of these shall be touched or taken from them. They shall not be forced to change their religion.\" This is recorded in the books of the Coptic Christians, the Synaxarium that I'm quoting to you from. \"Nor shall any of them be harmed, provided that they pay the jizya, once their harvest is gathered, as long as they abide by this treaty. The protection is a covenant under the responsibility of God and his prophet and the caliph.\"", "This is what Amr ibn al-As did. You see how ungrateful these people are? Imagine if Amr ibn al-As didn't come: probably Coptics would have been completely annihilated, because they would be forced to follow the specific sect of Cyrus and his specific beliefs, which he wanted to enforce from the Roman Empire, and not Coptic specific beliefs. So instead of them being grateful and saying thank you to the Muslims that came, liberated them (I wouldn't even use the word conquer them; liberate them, and this is no exaggeration, because Muslims came and this is what they did).", "Now look what also happened. It records the following: [Amr ibn al-As] [said] to the patriarch: \"Resume the government of your churches and your people, administering them as you wish.\" And when Benjamin told the Muslim caliph, or the leader appointed by the caliph, about the destruction of the church of Saint Mark, [Amr ibn al-As] granted him the sum of 10,000 dinars to begin the reconstruction of the sanctuary and the restoration of the monasteries in the desert. [Islam] came, liberated them; not only that, he's giving the patriarch money so you can reconstruct the church. This is in their own books.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Coptic Christian Warns The Western World From Islam And Muslims | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_iaUx01FVw", ((14, "0:14–1:32"), (92, "1:32–2:17"), (140, "2:20–3:09"), (189, "3:09–3:45"), (225, "3:45–4:15"), (255, "4:15–5:38"), (338, "5:38–6:12"), (372, "6:12–6:47"),), id: 3132)
