#passage(("Now he says Muslims destroyed the churches, all of this nonsense. Some of the oldest churches and [monasteries] in the world are in Egypt. You have, for example, the Hanging Church, from the third to fourth century. You have the Church of Abu Serga, fourth century. You have the Church of Saint Barbara, fourth to fifth century. You have the Church of the Holy Virgin, fourth century. You have the Great Basilica of Abu Mina, third to fourth century. You have Monastery of Saint Anthony, 356 AD. You have the Monastery of Saint Macarius, 360 AD. You have the Monastery of Saint Bishoy, 385. I can go on and on. Imagine this was the treatment, and all of these churches are left, and since the time of Amr ibn al-As Islam has been in Egypt. Why do we have these monasteries? So Muslims not only did they protect them, liberate them, give them freedom, allow the Pope to come back and rule and judge his people, but also all of these old sites, they remained. And someone says: \"Okay, what about today? They maybe persecuted today.\" No, today Sisi, the current Egyptian president, built them the biggest cathedral in the Middle East in 2019.", "From the 7th century, from Islam entering, up until, you can say, the 13th century, there was a Christian majority in Egypt, and they were living peacefully. Now, of course, a lot of people were accepting Islam gradually. So this majority was going down; it was still a majority up until the 13th century, but a lot of people were accepting Islam. Why? Because of that treatment from the laws, because of the honor that they were being given, and the goodness that they have been shown by the Fatimids and others, of course. So it was peaceful. Now, why did it start to change? Some tension started, and then things were not the same, starting, you can say, from around the 13th, 14th century. Why? Because of the Crusaders, because of all of the campaigns that have been released; a lot of riots have started in Egypt, a lot of issues have started.", "We have Al-Maqrizi. Al-Maqrizi is one of the foundational historians. Muslims and non-Muslims rely on his book called Khitat as a foundational text, because it's one of the biggest text that records the Mamluk history. And he describes the event. He described the issues: why did tension start? What was going on? A lot of them accepted. They said maybe like 60%, or barely above 50%, but they're still a majority. So, if they were a majority, what were they doing when the Crusaders started plundering and attacking Egypt and ports? Let's read what he tells us was happening: \"Christians of Egypt were looking towards the kings of the Franks, the Crusaders, expecting from them the conquest of the land and to end the Muslim rule.\" So, this is how they repay the Muslims. \"They maintain correspondence with them and inform them of the secrets of the Muslims and the weakness of their borders. Because of this, the hearts of the Muslims were filled with great rancor against them. They saw them as the enemy dwelling within their homes.\" So, what they're doing? They're living with you and they're sending information and secrets to the Crusaders.", "\"Whenever the news arrived that the Franks armies were moving towards the ports of Damietta or Alexandria, the common people and the soldiers became inflamed. They said: 'How can we fight an external enemy when there is an internal enemy within us?'\" (referring to the Coptic Christians who are giving all of these secrets and wishing for Muslims to be defeated) \"prospering our administration and wearing fine clothes.\" The Coptics were, by the way, in many high rankings in government within the Muslim Empire in Egypt. They were highly ranked in the highest ranks. They were Christians. So, the Muslims are now complaining: these people are in our highest ranks, and they want us dead, and they're helping the Crusaders. \"Thus, every victory of the Crusaders in the West brought a trial upon the Christians of the East. And the destruction of the churches was often a response to the movement of the Frankish fleets.\" \"The people complain to the Sultan, saying the Christian scribes were using their power in the government to favor the Franks and undermine the treasury of the Muslims. They argued that it was not fitting for those who sought the ruin of Islam by calling upon the Crusaders to be the one interested in this wealth.\"", "So, now we talk about the destruction of churches. We see now where the issue is starting. The issue is starting from the Crusaders, because the Crusaders were causing all of these riots. They were attacking the Muslims, and they were collaborating with the Christians in Egypt who were trying to betray the Muslims who were living amongst them for all of these years. Now there is an incident to do with churches. There was a sultan called Al-Nasir Muhammad, and he ordered a demolition for church in Zuhri pond, a newly built church near the citadel, because it was built without a legal permit. So he had a legal reason to demolish it. When he demolished the church, then you had the Christians, they didn't like it. So what did they do? This is what Al-Maqrizi says: \"A great fire broke out in the mosque of Az-Zahir.\" So, they burned the mosque because of a legal demolition. \"They [burned] a mosque and another mosques in the area of Al-Hakim. The flames grew so large that they could not be contained, and the people cried out in fear. Soon after, more fires appeared in the markets and the quarters of the city, and when the arsonists were caught, they were found to be Christians who had used chemicals to start blazes in the houses of God,\" in the mosques.", "So, we had all of these problems and riots happening because of the Crusades. But not only that: after this legal demolition of the church, you had all of these people burning mosques and burning markets. They were like gangs. So the common people said: \"If they burn our mosques, then we shall leave them no place to pray.\" So then you had Muslims burning the churches as a response. So, this is the historical chronological order. And then the sultan, when he sent to the patriarch, he addressed him with harsh words, and he said to him: \"These Christians are the ones who are doing these fires.\" Then the patriarch is like: \"They are fanatics who saw the destruction of the churches.\" What are you talking about? It was a legal demolition of one church, which was built without a legal permit. He said they were possessed by a spirit of vengeance and committed these deeds, \"but we do not approve of it.\" So, that was the history.", "So, why do you see that change? The biggest change in the population of Egypt, where violence or fights were happening, was due to the crusades. It was due to those Christians who were causing riots and fighting, and then the leaders had to fight against them. Everything I'm quoting to you now, I'm quoting from the books.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Coptic Christian Warns The Western World From Islam And Muslims | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_iaUx01FVw", ((407, "6:47–8:13"), (521, "8:41–9:30"), (570, "9:30–10:33"), (633, "10:33–11:39"), (700, "11:40–12:58"), (778, "12:58–13:47"), (827, "13:47–14:05"),), id: 3134)

