#passage(("You have some Muslims today who make the most ridiculous behaviours just to get more views and they say that it's for a good cause: \"I have to behave like this because we're competing with the Hollywood, this and that, so I'm going to behave in an outrageous way just to get views, to get Muslims to watch.\" And all you're doing is just feeding into this behavior of the Western world, you're helping moving people to the worst, because when you are doing all of these crazy things, trying to get attention of people to click on your videos, everyone is forced to do something similar to that because you're feeding into that, rather than making a group of people who follow you, who actually follow the Truth, who do not have to do all of this nonsense and all of this \"mumble-jumble\" and jumping up and down and acting like their Grandizer or something in order for them to get people to watch their content. If he's able to delete all of these videos and just simply make videos to bring people to his religion or what he believes is the Truth, why are certain Muslims not able to do these things?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Popular Youtuber Abandons Money And Fame For Christianity?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F3uZK_khCsk", ((563, "9:23–10:17"),), id: 3150)
// U ML283:249-265 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("I want to give this message to Muslims first. This is an example for you to understand. He, our brother in humanity, he might not be a Muslim, but what he's saying is essentially what the Quran and Sunnah are saying. So, what he's teaching there is definitely a good thing and he is making a positive impact. Even if he's leading people towards coming back to God or Christianity, it is much better following Christianity the right way, it is much better than where the West is going right now. So, he's doing good work even if he's not necessarily right. He's improving society in a way, the Western Society, by leading people to God or trying to do that which is good.", "So, his Fitra (his innate disposition), which we believe in as Muslims, something innate given by God to recognize the Truth, is there, has been awakened by that person who asked him that question about death, and then he said these things which are true, which are from the Fitra, because they're completely in line with the teachings of Islam. So, we need to take that as a lesson as Muslims. You're trying to become like some of those people who make millions of subscribers and views and fame and money, and you see those people, they are there, they're telling you, \"Where are you going to go? Where are you going to stack this money? Where is it going to go? In the end, you're going to die and that money is not going to come with you.\"",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Popular Youtuber Abandons Money And Fame For Christianity?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F3uZK_khCsk", ((1197, "19:57–20:35"), (1238, "20:38–21:04"),), id: 3151)
// U ML014:12-43 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("I think an issue that we have as a Muslim community is we do not have things that represent us as Muslims. So, for example, when you think about brands or you think about clothing brands, the first thing that comes to your mind is all of these brands that are made by non-Muslims, like Gucci and Armani and all of these brands. If you ask a Muslim, is there a Muslim clothing brand that does specifically Muslim clothing that is well-known, the answer would always be no. And this is one of the main reasons we are losing to the non-Muslims. Why are we losing? Because they have this monopoly on businesses, they have monopoly on social media, they have monopoly on so many things. And we are forced to use what they have and we're forced to represent what they have.", "When the issue in Palestine was going on, when it was heated, still going on until today, everybody was talking about boycotting so many of the brands that are supporting the evil entities that are doing what they're doing to our brothers and sisters. So, we as Muslims, we don't have something that represents us. We don't have something that we can go and be like, \"Okay, this is a brand that is a Muslim brand,\" that you look at this: \"I'm wearing clothing that represents me as a believer,\" not just wearing anything.", "The purpose behind this is to have some brand where we're able to have things that represents us as Muslims, instead of just supporting and buying things which are made by disbelievers, but rather supporting us on the things that we believe in as Muslims. This vision I have is not just to do with items that you can buy, but even apps and social media platforms and things like that. Step-by-step, inshallah, we're able to do things that are representing us, that we can have our own communities in, as many other communities are succeeding in by doing that we are not doing. And we're 2 billion people in the world.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “It's Finally Here!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eIn7iwVa8fg", ((34, "0:34–1:21"), (81, "1:21–1:54"), (135, "2:15–2:49"),), id: 3152)
// U ML027:594-651 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("It takes a bit of time, but we have to have a lot of honor in what we believe in. Born Muslims have these issues. They live in the West, and when you are in and around an environment that is non-Muslim, he's afraid to go to the boss and say, \"I want to pray,\" or \"I can't eat this food because it's halal,\" or \"I can't attend the celebration because it's Christmas and I'm a Muslim.\" They're afraid to say this. So, we have these issues as Muslims. Many Muslims have this issue where they feel inferior. It's an inferiority complex. It's sad, but it's the reality.", "I worked in this country. I told the guy, \"I have these five daily prayers. It's either you're going to allow me or I'm going to go find another job.\" Simple. The guy was like, \"Okay, yeah, sure. You're more than welcome to pray.\" But, people are not even asking. They're so afraid. They're so worried. I told him, \"I have a break. I'll take time from that break and in the day I will be praying. I'm not telling you give me free time. Let's say I have a 1-hour break. 30 minutes of it I'm going to extend when it comes to the prayer time and I'll just take a 30-minute break.\" He'll be happy. He doesn't have a problem. You're taking a break like he wants you to take a break, but you're praying at the same time. But, we have this inferiority complex, fear, whatever. You don't want to publicly say you're a Muslim because there's a lot of negative perceptions. Sad, but it is.", "A Muslim family living in a Muslim city is day and night different of how natural things are for you. Being in communities like this, it has a toll on the heart. Just being here, the minute I land I get depressed. But I got to do this. I got to come here and speak to the people who are not Muslims, cuz if you are in a Muslim country, as I live in a Muslim country, you're not going to invite people. So you need to go to the people who need guidance. You need to share the message with them.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “New Convert Questions Muslim On Lack Of Connection With God In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pwUuzBBM4o8", ((1518, "25:18–25:49"), (1549, "25:49–26:34"), (1611, "26:51–27:20"),), id: 3153)
// U ML250:29-49 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("First and foremost, people need to understand that as a Muslim, we have more damage and anger when these types of attacks happen than anyone else. And that might surprise some people, but this is the truth. People who talk about Islam in general, or try to tell people about Islam and the religion and its teachings, like I do on my channel, get the most frustration from these types of events, because at the end, an event like this happening from a 16-year-old kid ends up making all of this negative view, or image, or publicity, because it's misused by multiple media outlets and different individuals. So that actually frustrates us, because all of the work that we're doing of talking about Islam, and how beautiful it is as a religion, and how it teaches these good practices of being good to the people around you, then we get all of this negative publicity. That's number one.", "Number two, the lay Muslims, the general public, they end up dealing with the aftermath of this. They end up being treated differently. Whenever they're seen, people have prejudices against them just because of the religion, because a 16-year-old boy somewhere did something, which, as we said, we highly condemn. We have the most amount of problematic feelings when we see such things. So we would hate this more than anyone else would hate to see something like this, and I don't want anyone to think anything other than that. So we do condemn this in the strongest words possible.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Responding To @PBDPodcast Absurd Comments On The Bishop Incident! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=je-cOqAbR8c", ((130, "2:10–2:54"), (177, "2:57–3:26"),), id: 3154)
